The toggleVisible logic has a redundant ternary expression. When `item.visible === false`, it returns `true`, otherwise it returns `false`. This can be simplified to `!== false` since the condition handles the false case explicitly. However, the current implementation correctly handles undefined/missing visible properties by treating them as visible (setting visible to false on first toggle). Consider adding a comment to clarify this behavior, as the expression `item.visible === false ? true : false` is non-obvious.
```suggestion
            // Treat missing `visible` as visible; first toggle hides the item.
            const isCurrentlyVisible = item.visible !== false;
            return { ...item, visible: !isCurrentlyVisible };
```

The import error handler silently ignores invalid JSON without user feedback. While this prevents application crashes, users won't know why their import failed. Consider using the `navImportError` i18n key (already defined in all locales) to display an error message via toast, alert, or console.error to help users debug import issues.
```suggestion
        } catch (error) {
          // Surface invalid JSON errors to help users debug import issues
          // eslint-disable-next-line no-console
          console.error(t('navImportError'), error);
```
